": "Merit-Ptah, whose purported existence dates to approximately 2700 BCE during Egypt's Second Dynasty, stands as a pivotal, albeit now largely debunked, figure in the narrative of women in ancient medicine. For decades, she was widely celebrated as the world's first female physician and chief medical officer to a pharaoh's court, a claim that resonated deeply in feminist historical scholarship and popular culture. Her story, primarily based on a 1930s interpretation of an inscription from Saqqara, became a powerful symbol of early female professional achievement within a highly stratified ancient society, shaping perceptions of gender roles in antiquity and inspiring generations of women in medicine.

The widespread belief in Merit-Ptah originated from Canadian physician Kate Campbell Hurd-Mead's 1938 book, 'A History of Women in Medicine,' which cited an inscription on a tomb in the Saqqara necropolis, supposedly left by Merit-Ptah's son, a High Priest. This inscription was interpreted as referring to his mother as 'chief physician.' This account gained significant traction, especially in the mid-20th century, cementing Merit-Ptah's place in popular history books and as a namesake for medical awards and institutions, despite the lack of direct primary source verification.

However, subsequent rigorous Egyptological research, particularly by Dr. Jakub Kwecinski in the 21st century, meticulously demonstrated that no such inscription or historical record of a physician named Merit-Ptah exists. The name and title were likely misattributed or conflated with another ancient Egyptian female physician, Peseshet, who lived during the Fourth Dynasty (c. 2575–2465 BCE) and is genuinely attested as 'Directress of the Female Physicians.' This re-evaluation irrevocably altered the understanding of Merit-Ptah, transforming her from a historical figure into a powerful, enduring historical myth."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T23:15:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37076493495", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "2699 BCE, Old Kingdom of Egypt"}, {"title": "Medical challenges driving innovation in healing arts", "type": "Idea", "year": "2699 BCE, Old Kingdom of Egypt"}], "newValue": ["The 1938 publication of Kate Campbell Hurd-Mead's 'A History of Women in Medicine' introduced the story of Merit-Ptah based on a misinterpretation of an ancient inscription.", "The widespread desire to identify early female pioneers in traditionally male-dominated professions amplified the narrative of Merit-Ptah as the 'first female physician'.", "Limited access to and scholarly analysis of ancient Egyptian primary sources in the early 20th century allowed the misinterpretation to propagate unchallenged for decades."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T23:15:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37076493495", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Improved medical practice and public health outcomes", "type": "Idea", "year": "2659 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "2659 BCE"}], "newValue":